#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# pseudo_inverse

#% text
# `pseudo_inverse(m)` gives the Moore-Penrose pseudo-inverse of a matrix of numbers.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The pseudo-inverse `p` satisfies `m.p.m == m`, `p.m.p == p` and makes `m.p` and `p.m` Hermitian; for an invertible matrix it is the inverse.

#% item
# Exact matrices give exact results; machine matrices are computed from the singular value decomposition.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
pseudo_inverse([[1, 2], [3, 4]])
#% output
#= [[-2, 1], [3/2, -1/2]]

#% cell
pseudo_inverse([[1, 2], [2, 4]])
#% output
#= [[1/25, 2/25], [2/25, 4/25]]

#% cell
pseudo_inverse([[1.0, 0.0, 1.0], [0.0, 1.0, 1.0]])
#% output
#= [[0.6666666666666667, -0.3333333333333335], [-0.3333333333333335, 0.6666666666666667], [0.3333333333333333, 0.3333333333333333]]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The least-squares line through four points: the solution of the overdetermined system:

#% cell
a = [[1, 0], [1, 1], [1, 2], [1, 3]]; y = [1, 2, 2, 4]; dot(pseudo_inverse(a), y)
#% output
#= [9/10, 9/10]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The defining identity `m.p.m == m`:

#% cell
m = [[1, 2], [2, 4], [0, 1]]; p = pseudo_inverse(m); dot(m, p, m) == m
#% output
#= true

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `inverse`, `svd`, `fit`
